WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.660
Process invocation risk depends on both script design and operating context.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:10.760
A carefully written script can still have too much impact if it runs with broad administrative rights.

3
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:19.340
Automation identities, service accounts, file permissions, and delegated privilege rules should be scoped to the job they actually perform.

4
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.020
Least privilege reduces blast radius.

5
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:32.600
If an invoked process behaves unexpectedly, receives a malformed value, or encounters a tool behavior the script did not anticipate, narrower permissions limit what can be affected.

6
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.940
This is not a substitute for validation or safe argument passing.

7
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.480
It is another layer that makes mistakes less costly.

8
00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.640
External tools should be known and reviewed.

9
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:51.580
Teams should understand where tools come from, how versions are controlled, what output formats are relied upon, and how errors are reported.

10
00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:58.000
Helper scripts deserve the same review as the main script because they participate in the invocation chain.

11
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:04.920
Logging should record high-level actions, results, and failures without exposing secrets or sensitive data.

12
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:10.060
Good logs tell an operator what was attempted, what changed, and where to investigate next.

13
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:18.000
They should avoid printing tokens, private keys, full credential-bearing environment values, or sensitive command output.

14
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.640
Review practices make safe invocation sustainable.

15
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:25.100
ShellCheck can catch many Bash-specific issues.

16
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:32.880
Peer review can challenge assumptions about executable selection, environment, permissions, and error handling.

17
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:46.020
CI/CD linting, staged rollout, and realistic tests with missing files, unexpected paths, spaced values, and dependency failures help catch problems before scripts become trusted production automation.
